-- url     : https://prove2.me/theorems/0c509178-3031-57ce-91f3-586ef51fd84c
-- title:
--   Non-zero limit of (σ-1)Λ(σ) and blocks at σ=1
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $\mathbb{A}$ denotes the adele ring of $\mathbb{Q}$ and $G = \mathrm{GL}_3(\mathbb{A})$ (in Lean, `AdelicGL 3 (𝓞 ℚ) ℚ`). The fixed data are: a finite set $S$ of nonzero primes of $\mathbb{Z}$ (height-one primes of $\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{Q}}$), a group homomorphism $\omega : \mathbb{A}^{\times} \to \mathbb{C}^{\times}$, two functions $\lambda_1, \lambda_2$ from primes to $\mathbb{C}$, and a function $\Lambda : \mathbb{R} \to [0,\infty]$.
--
--   Five groups of notions recur in the hypotheses and the conclusion, and are named here once.
--
--   (i) *Whittaker transform.* For $\varphi : G \to \mathbb{C}$ and $g \in G$, $W_\varphi(g)$ denotes `whittaker3` for the pins `productionPinsOf ℚ ∅ (fun _ => ⊥) (fun _ => 1) (AdelicBox.adelicBox ℚ)` and the character [`NumberField.StandardAddChar.psiQ`](def/NumberField_StandardGlobalAddCharRat.html#L615), that is the iterated integral $\int\!\!\int\!\!\int \varphi(u(x,y,z)\,g)\,\psi(-(x+y))$, where $u(x,y,z)$ is the upper unipotent matrix `upperUnipotent3` with entries $x, y, z$, $\psi$ is the standard additive character of $\mathbb{A}$ assembled from the local characters at the infinite and finite places, and each of the three integrations is against the measure carried by those pins, namely the adelic additive Haar measure conditioned on the box `AdelicBox.adelicBox ℚ` (infinite part in a fundamental domain for the lattice of the mixed embedding, finite part the integral finite adeles). From the pins `whittaker3` uses only this measurable space and measure; the remaining fields there are $D = \emptyset$, $U \equiv \bot$ and $\mathrm{gen} \equiv 1$.
--
--   (ii) *Integrals over the unipotent quotient.* Let $X$ be the orbit quotient of $G$ by the subgroup `unipotentSubgroup3` generated by all $u(x,y,z)$, with the measure [`WhittakerBlock.quotientMeasure`](def/LanglandsTunnell_CubicInduction_WhittakerBlock.html#L28) obtained by the Haar-quotient construction from the Haar measure of $G$ and the pushforward to that subgroup of the threefold product of adelic additive Haar measures; for $q \in X$, $q.\mathrm{out}$ is a chosen representative in $G$. For $\varphi : G \to \mathbb{C}$, $\Phi : \mathbb{A}^3 \to \mathbb{C}$ and $\sigma \in \mathbb{R}$ put
--   $$F_{\varphi,\Phi,\sigma}(q) \;=\; \|W_\varphi(q.\mathrm{out})\|^2 \cdot \|\Phi(e_3\, q.\mathrm{out})\| \cdot \|\det q.\mathrm{out}\|^{\sigma} \;\in\; [0,\infty],$$
--   where $e_3\,q.\mathrm{out}$ is the bottom row $j \mapsto (q.\mathrm{out})_{2j}$ of the representative matrix and $\|\cdot\|$ on the determinant is [`NumberField.TateGlobal.ideleNorm`](def/NumberField_TateGlobalZeta.html#L19) (the module of an idele, defined through `distribHaarChar`). Write $Q(\varphi,\Phi,\sigma)$ for the $[0,\infty]$-valued integral $\int^{-} F_{\varphi,\Phi,\sigma}$ over all of $X$, and $B(\varphi,\Phi,\sigma) =$ [`WhittakerBlock.block … φ Φ σ S`](def/LanglandsTunnell_CubicInduction_WhittakerBlock.html#L40) for the same integral restricted to the set `zerothShell S` of those $q$ whose component at every prime $p \notin S$ can be written $u(x,y,z)\,k$ with $k$ in `localMaximalCompact3` (the subgroup of matrices over $\mathbb{Q}_p$ all of whose entries, and all of whose inverse's entries, have valuation $\le 1$).
--
--   (iii) *Eigenform conditions $E(\varphi)$ for the data $(S,\omega,\lambda_1,\lambda_2)$:* $\varphi$ is continuous; $\varphi(\gamma g) = \varphi(g)$ for $\gamma$ in the image of $\mathrm{GL}_3(\mathbb{Q})$ under `globalPointsGL`; $\varphi(z g) = \omega(z)\varphi(g)$ for $z$ in the image of $\mathbb{A}^{\times}$ under `centralScalarGL`; for every $p \notin S$, $\varphi$ is right invariant under the image in $G$ of `localMaximalCompact3` at $p$; and for every $p \notin S$, $\varphi$ is a coset eigenfunction, in the sense of `IsCosetEigenfunction` (for every finite system of representatives of the cosets of the relevant double coset, the sum of the translates of $\varphi$ by the representatives equals the eigenvalue times $\varphi$), for the image of `heckeGen1 p` $= \mathrm{diag}(\varpi_p,1,1)$ with eigenvalue $\lambda_1(p)$ and for the image of `heckeGen2 p` $= \mathrm{diag}(\varpi_p,\varpi_p,1)$ with eigenvalue $\lambda_2(p)$.
--
--   (iv) *Admissibility of $\Phi$, written $A(\Phi)$:* there are functions $\Phi_0,\Phi_1,\Phi_2 : \mathbb{A} \to \mathbb{C}$, each lying in [`NumberField.AdelicFourier.pureTensorSet ℚ`](def/NumberField_AdelicFourier.html#L75) (a product of a Schwartz function on the mixed space at the infinite places with a locally constant, compactly supported function on the finite adeles) and each factorizable and standard outside $S$ in the sense of `IsFactorizableStandardOutside` (equal to the indicator of the set of adeles integral outside $S$ times a product of local factors, arbitrary at the infinite places and at the places of $S$), with $\Phi(x) = \prod_i \Phi_i(x_i)$; moreover $\Phi$ takes values in the non-negative reals pointwise ($0 \le \operatorname{Re}\Phi(x)$ and $\operatorname{Im}\Phi(x) = 0$), and $\int \operatorname{Re}\Phi > 0$ for the product of three copies of the adelic additive Haar measure on $\mathbb{A}^3$.
--
--   (v) *Translation data.* A triple $(n, c, t)$ consists of $n \in \mathbb{N}$, scalars $c : \mathrm{Fin}\,n \to \mathbb{C}$ and elements $t : \mathrm{Fin}\,n \to G$ such that each $t_i$ has trivial archimedean component (`archComponent3`) and trivial component at every prime $p \notin S$ (`componentAt3`); the associated combination is $\varphi_{c,t} : x \mapsto \sum_i c_i\, f(x\, t_i)$ for the relevant $f$.
--
--   The hypotheses are as follows. The hypothesis `hΛ` has two clauses: $1 \le \Lambda(\sigma)$ for every real $\sigma$; and, for every $f : G \to \mathbb{C}$ satisfying $E(f)$, every translation datum $(n,c,t)$, every $\Phi$ with $A(\Phi)$ and every real $\sigma$, the shell-factor identity $Q(\varphi_{c,t},\Phi,\sigma) = \Lambda(\sigma)\, B(\varphi_{c,t},\Phi,\sigma)$. Next, `adm` is an abstract predicate on functions $G \to \mathbb{C}$ and `hadm` states that $\mathrm{adm}\,\varphi$ implies $E(\varphi)$. Next, $I$ assigns an element of $[0,\infty]$ to a triple $(\varphi,\Phi,\sigma)$ and $V \in [0,\infty]$ is a constant, and `hD` states that for every $\varphi$ with $\mathrm{adm}\,\varphi$, every $\Phi$ with $A(\Phi)$ and every $\sigma \in (1,2]$ one has $I(\varphi,\Phi,\sigma) = V \cdot Q(\varphi,\Phi,\sigma)$. Next, $\ell$ assigns an element of $[0,\infty]$ to a pair $(\varphi,\Phi)$, and `hG` states that for $\mathrm{adm}\,\varphi$ and $A(\Phi)$ the functions $\sigma \mapsto (\sigma-1)\,I(\varphi,\Phi,\sigma)$ converge to $\ell(\varphi,\Phi)$ as $\sigma \to 1$ within $(1,\infty)$, while `hℓ` states that under the same assumptions $\ell(\varphi,\Phi) \ne \infty$, and $\ell(\varphi,\Phi) \ne 0$ whenever $\varphi \ne 0$. Finally a function $f : G \to \mathbb{C}$ is fixed, `hadm₀` states that $\mathrm{adm}\,\varphi_{c,t}$ holds for every translation datum $(n,c,t)$, and `hU1` asserts the existence of a translation datum $(n,c,t)$ with $\varphi_{c,t} \ne 0$, of a $\Phi$ with $A(\Phi)$, and of a constant $C \ne \infty$ with $B(\varphi_{c,t},\Phi,\sigma) \le C$ for all $\sigma \in (1,2]$.
--
--   The conclusion asserts the existence of $L \in [0,\infty]$ with $L \ne 0$ and $L \ne \infty$ such that $\sigma \mapsto (\sigma-1)\,\Lambda(\sigma)$ (formed in $[0,\infty]$ as `ENNReal.ofReal (σ - 1) * Λ σ`) converges to $L$ as $\sigma \to 1$ within $(1,\infty)$, and such that for every $\varphi$ with $\mathrm{adm}\,\varphi$ and every $\Phi$ with $A(\Phi)$ both of the following hold: the function $\sigma \mapsto B(\varphi,\Phi,\sigma)$ converges to $B(\varphi,\Phi,1)$ as $\sigma \to 1$ within $(1,\infty)$; and $B(\varphi,\Phi,1) \cdot (V \cdot L) = \ell(\varphi,\Phi)$. The last two conjuncts are asserted for all admissible pairs $(\varphi,\Phi)$, with no non-vanishing assumption on $\varphi$, and in particular for $\varphi$ not of the form $\varphi_{c,t}$.
--
--   This is the residue step in the Rankin–Selberg analysis on $\mathrm{GL}_3$ used in the Langlands–Tunnell part of the development: a single bounded Whittaker block for one non-zero translate combination of $f$ pins down the finite non-zero limit $L$ of $(\sigma-1)\Lambda(\sigma)$, and thereby identifies the block at exponent $\sigma = 1$, for all admissible data, as $\ell(\varphi,\Phi)/(V L)$. It is cited by the $L^2$ slab statements about smoothing kernels and expansions of the Whittaker transform.
